# Analog Electronics-1 (2026)

## Assignment 3: Full-Wave Rectifier Simulation Using LTspice

*Student Name:* Hashir Khan  
*Tool Used:* LTspice  
*Simulation Type:* Transient Analysis  

---

## Objective
This assignment simulates a *full-wave rectifier circuit* using LTspice and analyses its waveform behavior.

The circuit converts an AC sine wave input into a pulsating DC output using ideal diodes. A capacitor filter is then added to smooth the output into a more stable DC voltage.

---

## Simulation Parameters
- *Input Source:* SINE(0 100 50)
- *Peak Voltage:* 100 V
- *Frequency:* 50 Hz
- *Transient Stop Time:* 40 ms
- *Load Resistance:* 2.2 kΩ
- *Filter Capacitor:* 470 µF

---

## Submitted Files
- LTspice schematic file (.asc)
- Screenshot 1: Schematic window
- Screenshot 2: Simulation plot
- Screenshot 3: Capacitor filtered output
- README.md

---

## Analysis of the Plot
The waveform plot shows:

- *Input Voltage (Vin):* sinusoidal AC waveform
- *Output Voltage (Vout):* full-wave rectified waveform
- Both positive and negative half cycles are converted into positive pulses
- After adding the capacitor, the output becomes *smooth DC with very small ripple*

This verifies the theoretical behavior of a *bridge rectifier with capacitor filter*.

---

## Observation
Without capacitor:
- output is pulsating DC
- voltage drops to zero between peaks

With capacitor:
- capacitor charges to peak voltage
- slowly discharges through load resistor
- ripple becomes very small
- output becomes nearly constant DC

---

## Learning Outcome
Through this assignment, I learned how a full-wave bridge rectifier works in LTspice simulation. I also understood the difference between pulsating DC and filtered DC using a capacitor smoothing circuit.
